          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-d16e0b33503dd1b5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"The utterance is primarily a direct, concessively phrased avowal that the speaker does love the addressee. His immediate contrast—“But I want it to be something else”—separates that acknowledged state from the further conjunction or pledge he desires, so the line itself does not substantially undertake a commitment. Its grim delivery indicates emotional tension but supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Because the avowal occurs within a negotiation over their relationship, it has some interpersonal consequence, but the wording explicitly distinguishes love from the further relational undertaking the speaker seeks.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly spoken avowal by the male speaker to the woman who has asked him to say that he loves her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The woman solicits the declaration, but the speaker answers grimly and with the concessive “right enough.” He acknowledges love while rejecting her claim that love alone should constitute their relationship; he wants a different or further form of conjunction. This makes the line a sincere-seeming but resistant avowal rather than an enacted pledge or affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you right enough,” he said, grimly.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense declaration, strengthened by “right enough,” supports a truth-conditional avowal; “grimly” marks tension but not independently expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“But I want it to be something else.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate qualification distinguishes his acknowledged love from the different relational condition he wants, weighing against performative commitment in the highlighted utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She first pleads, “Say you love me,” and later asks, “Why isn’t it enough?”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration is a considered response within an argument about whether love is sufficient, rather than a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier, he thinks, “If she really could pledge herself,” while their dispute contrasts love with the conjunction or pledge he seeks.\",\"supports\":\"The passage explicitly locates commitment in a further pledge, not in the bare love avowal itself.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the concessive avowal. The surrounding relational struggle and grim delivery can be represented contextually without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
